Why the weights are not MIT
---------------------------

The model is trained on two sources with very different terms.

USGS 3DEP is a work of the US federal government and carries no copyright
(17 U.S.C. § 105). Citation is a courtesy the USGS asks for, not a condition,
and it is given above.

OpenStreetMap is under ODbL 1.0, which is share-alike, and that is the part
that needs a stated position. OSM geometry is used here as *training labels*.
It is not redistributed, and the model does not consult OSM at inference time:
it reads bare-earth elevation and emits a probability raster.

ODbL distinguishes a Derivative Database, which must itself be ODbL, from a
Produced Work, which may be licensed freely provided it carries attribution.
Which of the two a set of neural network weights is has not been settled — the
OSM Foundation has issued no guideline that squarely answers it, and reasonable
people in the community disagree.

This project's position is that the weights are a Produced Work. The model
cannot be queried for OSM features, reproduces no OSM geometry, and yields a
raster rather than data. But that position is an argument, not a ruling, so the
weights are released under a share-alike licence anyway. If the Produced Work
reading is right, CC BY-SA gives away more freedom than required. If it is
wrong, the share-alike obligation ODbL would have imposed has been honoured
regardless. Being wrong in that direction costs this project nothing and costs
the OSM community nothing, which is the only asymmetry that matters here.

CC BY-SA rather than ODbL itself because ODbL is built around database rights
and applies awkwardly to something that is not a database. CC BY-SA rather than
plain CC BY — the choice TrailScan made for its weights — because ODbL is
share-alike and dropping that on the way through is exactly the move the
licence exists to prevent.


What this means in practice
---------------------------

  * Use the weights, including commercially: yes.
  * Fine-tune them, or distil them into another model: yes, and the result is
    also CC BY-SA 4.0.
  * Bundle them in a proprietary product: yes, but the weights stay CC BY-SA
    and the attribution above must be shown.
  * Trace the heatmap into OpenStreetMap: yes — that is what it is for. See
    the mapper guidance in README.md, which is about OSM's expectations rather
    than about copyright.
